This career was listed as one of my “Best” assessment matches.
What people in this career actually do day-to-day:
Network administrators install, configure, maintain, and troubleshoot computer networks and
servers. They may manage LANs and WANs, install updates, control user access, maintain
network security, back up systems, and fix network or hardware problems.
Work environment:
They usually work inside organizations with other IT professionals. Employers include
technology companies, schools, financial institutions, healthcare organizations, manufacturers,
and government agencies. Most work full time, although nights or weekends may sometimes be
necessary for maintenance or emergencies.
Required education and training:
A bachelor's degree in information technology, cybersecurity, computer science, or a related
field is typical. Some employers accept an associate degree or professional certifications
combined with experience.
Useful certifications could include:
CompTIA Network+
CompTIA Security+
Cisco CCNA
Microsoft Azure certifications
AWS certifications
Key skills and abilities required:
Five important O*NET skills include:
Active Listening
Critical Thinking
Monitoring
Reading Comprehension
Speaking
Other important abilities include troubleshooting, systems analysis, complex problem solving,
and programming.
Salary range:
Entry-level: approximately $62,640
Median: $99,130
Experienced: approximately $155,050+
Job outlook:
Declining
Projected growth rate: -4% from 2025–2035
Even with the decline, BLS projects around 13,400 openings each year, largely because
workers will change careers or retire. Automation, cloud services, DevOps, and
Network-as-a-Service are expected to reduce some traditional administrator duties.
