Security Manager was also listed as a “Great” match in my CareerOneStop results.
What people in this career actually do day-to-day:
Security Managers oversee an organization's security operations. They may manage
employees, investigate security breaches, create emergency plans, oversee security systems,
develop budgets, enforce security policies, and protect employees, property, and facilities.
Work environment:
Security Managers may work in corporate offices, government facilities, military or defense
environments, hospitals, warehouses, universities, or large industrial facilities. The role involves
both office work and interacting with employees, security personnel, contractors, and leadership.
Required education and training:
Requirements vary, but management positions commonly require a bachelor's degree plus
previous security, military, law-enforcement, facilities, or management experience. Related
certifications in physical security, emergency management, or security management may
strengthen a candidate.
Key skills and abilities required:
According to O*NET, five of the most important skills are:
Critical Thinking
Active Listening
Reading Comprehension
Monitoring
Speaking
Important management skills also include judgment and decision making, complex problem
solving, coordination, and personnel management.
